BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-01-03-earth-at-perihelion@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260103
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260104
SUMMARY:Earth at perihelion
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: No direct visual change\; solar disk slightly larger\n\nEarth is closest to the Sun on 2026-01-03 (about 17:16 UTC)\; seasons are still controlled mainly by axial tilt.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:No direct visual change\; solar disk slightly larger
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-01-10-jupiter-at-opposition@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260110
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260111
SUMMARY:Jupiter at opposition
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: Excellent evening and night visibility\n\nJupiter is bright and high in the winter sky\; good telescope target.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:Excellent evening and night visibility
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-02-19-mercury-at-greatest-eastern-elongation@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260219
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260220
SUMMARY:Mercury at greatest eastern elongation
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: Best 2026 evening apparition from northern Europe\n\nLook low in the west after sunset\; crescent Moon\, Mercury\, Venus and Saturn are nearby around Feb 18-20.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:Best 2026 evening apparition from northern Europe
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-06-09-venus-jupiter-conjunction@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260609
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260610
SUMMARY:Venus-Jupiter conjunction
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: Low in the western evening twilight\n\nThe two brightest planets appear about 1.6 degrees apart\; clear western horizon needed.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:Low in the western evening twilight
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-07-06-earth-at-aphelion@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260707
SUMMARY:Earth at aphelion
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: No direct visual change\; solar disk slightly smaller\n\nEarth reaches its farthest point from the Sun on 2026-07-06 (about 17:31 UTC)\; seasonal weather is still driven mainly by axial tilt.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:No direct visual change\; solar disk slightly smaller
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-09-19-venus-at-peak-evening-visibility@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260919
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260920
SUMMARY:Venus at peak evening visibility
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: Very bright evening star\n\nGood evening visibility around this date\; Venus is prominent after sunset.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:Very bright evening star
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-10-04-saturn-at-opposition@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261004
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261005
SUMMARY:Saturn at opposition
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: Visible all night\n\nSaturn is brightest and best placed for observing\; rings appear slowly opening again.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:Visible all night
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-11-15-mars-jupiter-conjunction@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261115
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:Mars-Jupiter conjunction
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: Morning sky\n\nMars and Jupiter appear close together before dawn.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:Morning sky
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:planetenuhr-en-planet-2026-11-29-venus-at-peak-morning-visibility@marsrakete.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260805T170001Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261129
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261130
SUMMARY:Venus at peak morning visibility
CATEGORIES:PLANET
DESCRIPTION:Sichtbarkeit: Bright morning star\n\nVenus becomes a strong pre-dawn object.
X-PLANETENUHR-VISIBILITY:Bright morning star
END:VEVENT
